<UserControl x:Class="WaveFlume.App.Views.HelpView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <ScrollViewer Margin="8" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
        <StackPanel MaxWidth="1000" HorizontalAlignment="Left">
            <GroupBox Header="About">
                <StackPanel>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Margin="0,0,0,4">
                        <Image Source="/Assets/WaveFlume-128.png" Width="48" Height="48" Margin="0,0,10,0" RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="HighQuality"/>
                        <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" FontSize="18" VerticalAlignment="Center" Text="Wave Flume"/>
                    </StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="VersionText" Style="{StaticResource Muted}" Margin="0,0,0,6"/>
                    <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap">
                        Control program for a 3.0 m x 45.0 m wave flume with six independent paddles. You describe a wave,
                        the program works out the paddle motion and checks that it is safe, sends it to the controller,
                        runs it, and records what the wave probes measure. It makes regular waves (Monochromatic, Bichromatic, Cnoidal),
                        a single Solitary wave, Focusing wave groups, irregular sea states (Random: Pierson-Moskowitz, Generalised PM,
                        JONSWAP, TMA or Darbyshire spectrum), and waves or paddle motions you supply in a file.
                    </TextBlock>
                </StackPanel>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="Safety">
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap">
                    This PC supervises the flume but is not a real-time safety system. The hardwired emergency stop is the
                    main way to stop the paddles; the on-screen EMERGENCY STOP is an additional request. The controller and
                    drives apply their own stroke, speed and acceleration limits independently of this program.
                </TextBlock>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="User Manual - how to run a test">
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" xml:space="preserve">First time only:
   Settings tab: enter the Max Acceleration of the drive, then press Save Settings. Until this is done no wave can be sent.
   Anyone using the program can change the settings; there is no login.

Every test:
   1. Wave Generation tab: choose the Wave Type, fill in the numbers, press Calculate. The Safety Gatekeeper box must not be red.
   2. (Optional) Type a Test Number and press Save to keep the wave as a recipe.
   3. Wave Paddle tab: press Connect, Enable Servos, Starting Position, Send to Wave Paddle, then Start Wave, in that order.
   4. Press Smooth Stop to end the run. Press EMERGENCY STOP in an emergency.
   5. The data (CSV) and the report (PDF) are saved automatically in the Runs folder.

A button that is greyed out is not allowed yet. Each step unlocks the next.</TextBlock>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="Wave types">
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" xml:space="preserve">Monochromatic: regular waves of one height and one period. Choose 1st order, or 2nd order for a more accurate wave shape.
   Inputs: Water Depth, Wave Height, Wave Period, Number of Waves, Ramp Duration, Wave Angle (0 = straight wave).

Random: an irregular sea state. Height is the significant wave height Hs, period is the peak period Tp.
   Spectral Shape: Generalised PM (set Beta; 1.25 is the standard Pierson-Moskowitz) or JONSWAP (set Gamma; 3.3 is standard).
   Random Seed: the same seed gives exactly the same sea every time; a different seed gives a different sea.
   Scale realisation to target Hs: adjusts the random sea so its measured Hs equals the Hs you typed.
   Use at least 100 waves for statistics that are representative of the sea state.</TextBlock>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="Wave Paddle buttons">
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" xml:space="preserve">Connect: opens the link to the controller (the same button then reads Disconnect).
Enable Servos: switches on the servo drives.
Starting Position: moves the paddles to their starting (zero) position, so that zero on the screen is zero on the machine. Needed once after the servos are switched on.
Send to Wave Paddle: sends the calculated wave to the controller (the blue bar shows progress).
Start Wave: starts the wave. Smooth Stop: ends it gently. EMERGENCY STOP: stops at once.
Reset Fault: clears a fault after the cause has been removed.
Save Test Data / PDF Report / Open Test Data: keep, report on, or review a recorded run.</TextBlock>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="If the wave is BLOCKED (red)">
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" xml:space="preserve">The program never sends a wave that would break the limits. Read the red message, then:
   Wave too high or too steep (breaking)  ->  lower the height, lengthen the period or increase the water depth.
   Stroke, velocity or acceleration too large  ->  lower the height or lengthen the period.
   Value outside the allowed range  ->  type a value inside the range shown in the message.
   Acceleration limit not configured  ->  enter the Max Acceleration of the drive in Settings and press Save Settings.
The Limit plot on the Wave Generation tab shows the highest wave allowed for every period.</TextBlock>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="Alarms">
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" xml:space="preserve">An alarm shows as a coloured bar under the title. Press Details to see what the problem is and what to do about it.
Press Acknowledge in that window once you have read it. All alarms are also listed in the Alarm / event log at the bottom.
   Yellow = warning (the run can continue).  Red = critical or emergency stop (the paddles have been told to stop).</TextBlock>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="Where your files are saved">
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" xml:space="preserve">Recipes:  recipes.json in the data folder (Documents\WaveFlume by default; it can be changed in Settings).
Runs:  the Runs folder inside the data folder. Each run gives a CSV and a PDF report named date_time_TestNumber.
The date and time in the file name are UTC.
Settings and program logs:  %LOCALAPPDATA%\WaveFlume.</TextBlock>
            </GroupBox>

            <GroupBox Header="Full manual">
                <StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,6">
                        The complete User Manual, with pictures and every setting explained, is a PDF.
                    </TextBlock>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                        <Button x:Name="OpenManualButton" Content="Open the User Manual (PDF)" Padding="14,4" Click="OnOpenManual"/>
                        <TextBlock x:Name="OnlineLinkText" Visibility="Collapsed" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="16,0,0,0">
                            <Hyperlink x:Name="OnlineLink" Click="OnOnlineLink">Download the User Manual online</Hyperlink>
                        </TextBlock>
                    </StackPanel>
                    <TextBlock x:Name="ManualMissingText" Visibility="Collapsed" Style="{StaticResource Muted}" Margin="0,6,0,0" TextWrapping="Wrap"/>
                </StackPanel>
            </GroupBox>
        </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
